# =====================================================================
# Qwen 3.6 35B-A3B (MoE, IQ4_XS) @ 260000 ctx / 2 slots — 2026-07-28
# Service: uap-llama-server.service -> scripts/run-llama-server-continuity.sh
# Install: cp to ~/.config/uap/llama-server.env.qwen36-35b-a3b
#
# CONTEXT BUDGET — the reason parallel is 2 and not 1 or 3
# llama.cpp treats --ctx-size as the TOTAL across slots; each slot gets
# ctx_size / parallel. Delivery tuning holds a session at <130k to stay in the
# model's competent range (PROXY_CONTEXT_WINDOW=130000), so any KV beyond 130k
# in a single slot is unreachable — it can never be filled.
#
#   parallel 1 -> 1 x 260000  : 130000 of KV permanently idle
#   parallel 2 -> 2 x 130000  : exact fit, 2 concurrent sessions  <-- optimal
#   parallel 3 -> 3 x  86666  : each slot now BELOW the 130k target
#
# So 2 is not a compromise, it is the only division that both fills the KV and
# keeps every slot at the target window. Raising ctx to 390000 for 3x130000 was
# tried previously and OOMs on this card.
#
# Keep PROXY_CONCURRENCY_LIMIT equal to the slot count. If the proxy admits more
# concurrent contexts than there are slots, the extra one evicts a resident slot
# and that costs a FULL prompt reprocess — far worse than queueing.
# =====================================================================
LLAMA_BIN=/home/cogtek/llama.cpp/.worktrees/latest-master/build/bin/llama-server
LLAMA_MODEL=/home/cogtek/Downloads/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-IQ4_XS.gguf
# Model id advertised to API clients (llama-server --alias). Set it EXPLICITLY
# here: the script's fallback derives a name from the GGUF filename, which is a
# name rather than a path but is not the name client configs are written
# against ("Qwen3.8-27B-UD-IQ4_XS" vs "Qwen3.8-27B"), so the proxy would still
# rewrite the model on every request.
# ORDER IS NOT PRESERVED: llama.cpp stores aliases in a std::set and reports
# *begin() as the model id, so the ASCII-FIRST entry becomes the primary id.
# Keep the id clients actually use sorting first if you add more.
LLAMA_ALIAS=Qwen3.6-35B-A3B
LLAMA_MMPROJ=/home/cogtek/Downloads/mmproj-F16.gguf

LLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0
LLAMA_PORT=8080
LLAMA_CTX_SIZE=260000
LLAMA_PARALLEL=2
LLAMA_THREADS=16
LLAMA_GPU_LAYERS=99

# Qwen tolerates q4_0 KV well (unlike Gemma) and this model is large — 17.7 GB
# of weights — so the cheap KV is what makes 260000 total fit at all.
LLAMA_CACHE_TYPE_K=q4_0
LLAMA_CACHE_TYPE_V=q4_0

LLAMA_BATCH_SIZE=512
LLAMA_UBATCH_SIZE=512

# Speculative decoding OFF, deliberately. A Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-IQ4_XS-MTP.gguf
# exists on disk, but this is a MoE (A3B = ~3B active/token) and the Gemma 26B
# A4B A/B on this same box showed MTP is a no-op on MoE while costing ~2 GB
# (draft head + its own f16 draft KV). Do not wire it without re-measuring.
# Draft-model spec also crashes the Qwen3.x hybrid-memory (GDN) seq_add path.
LLAMA_ENABLE_SPEC_DECODING=false
LLAMA_SPEC_TYPE=none
LLAMA_DRAFT_MAX=3
LLAMA_DRAFT_MIN=1
LLAMA_DRAFT_P_MIN=0.75

LLAMA_REPEAT_PENALTY=1.0
LLAMA_CACHE_REUSE=256
LLAMA_SLOT_SAVE_PATH=/home/cogtek/.cache/inference/kv-slots
LLAMA_LOG_FILE=/home/cogtek/llama.cpp/llama-server.log
LLAMA_N_PREDICT=16384

# CHAT TEMPLATE (2026-08-20): switched from qwen3.5-enhanced.jinja to
# qwen-sharp.jinja -- peculiar-ragdoll/Qwen-Sharp-Chat-Templates
# (template_version qwen3.8-froggeric-v22.1), vendored verbatim at
# tools/agents/config/qwen-sharp.jinja. It targets Qwen 3.5/3.6/3.8 and is the
# template for ALL qwen models here. Pinned artifact -- both are asserted in
# test/chat-template-system-message.test.ts so a silent re-vendor fails CI:
#   upstream revision  3dc34df52c63dd22ada21f96435e069deaa8d7da (2026-08-18)
#   file sha256        d1f22a89eac3609dcfaa7b471b1f7d23bee2f084d275d26f4f8231d1d7908f4e
#
# WHAT IT FIXES: qwen3.5-enhanced.jinja hard-raised
# 'No user query found in messages.' -- a server-side failure for the whole
# request -- whenever no user turn survived its backwards scan, i.e. when every
# user turn was a <tool_response> or when the transcript was system+assistant
# only. Both shapes occur in normal agent loops. qwen-sharp renders them.
#
# WHAT STAYS COMPATIBLE (rendered and compared, not assumed):
#   * the tool-call contract the model is told to EMIT is unchanged:
#     <tool_call><function=NAME><parameter=K>V</parameter></function></tool_call>.
#     anthropic_proxy.py's _HERMES_FUNCTION_RE / _TOOL_CALL_XML_RE parse it
#     unchanged. The default is selected at render time by `_tool_format`, so
#     the test asserts on RENDERED output -- the file contains a JSON emitter
#     too, and a grep cannot tell which one is live.
#   * the generation prompt matches the old template in BOTH enable_thinking
#     states, so --reasoning auto --reasoning-format deepseek is unaffected.
#   * a mid-conversation system message renders inline in both templates.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY DIFFERS -- known and accepted:
#   * HISTORY rendering of prior tool calls differs in whitespace only: with
#     string-form `arguments` (the proxy's path -- it json.dumps them) the old
#     template emitted "}\n</function>", the new one "}</function>"; and the
#     separator between two tool calls in one assistant turn went "\n" ->
#     "\n\n". Neither changes what the parsers accept.
#   * the injected tool-calling instruction block was rewritten upstream: it now
#     requires a <think> block before each tool call and forbids prose before
#     the call. Behavioural, deliberate, not a wire change.
#   * a system message containing an IMAGE now raises. Unreachable via the
#     proxy, which flattens system to a text string before dispatch
#     (anthropic_proxy.py ~4709). Direct-to-:8080 OpenAI clients can hit it.
#   * unknown message roles no longer raise; they render as "[role]: content".
#   * prior <think> blocks are retained across turns. Upstream sells this as a
#     prompt-cache win; on the PROXY path it is a no-op, because the proxy
#     already strips <think> from prior assistant turns on purpose
#     (anthropic_proxy.py ~4725: "Stripping breaks the copy cycle"). On the
#     direct path it re-introduces exactly that cycle -- pass
#     chat_template_kwargs.preserve_thinking=false there if it shows up.
#
# chat_template_kwargs.reasoning_effort accepts
# none|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh|max. NOTE: 'none' forces thinking OFF,
# overriding enable_thinking=true.
# Do NOT switch this to "embedded" -- the model's own baked-in template still
# carries the 'System message must be at the beginning.' raise.
LLAMA_CHAT_TEMPLATE_FILE=/home/cogtek/dev/miller-tech/universal-agent-protocol/tools/agents/config/qwen-sharp.jinja

# --cache-ram sizing — measured, not guessed
# The host-RAM prompt cache holds KV prefixes so a follow-up turn re-uses its
# prompt instead of re-prefilling it. Undersize it and it thrashes: every
# eviction turns the next turn on that prefix into a full reprocess.
#
# Measured on this box at 16384 (2026-07-29), one hour of a live agent session:
#   27 evictions, ~23.5 GB churned  (avg entry 869 MiB)
#   30 large reprocesses >5k tokens, 829,587 tokens re-prefilled
#   418s of the 482s total prefill time — 87% — spent re-doing work
#   worst single stall 44.4s
# The eviction count and the large-reprocess count track ~1:1, which is the
# causal link: the cache was holding a working set 1.5x its size.
#
# 32768 (32 GiB) clears that ~23.5 GB working set with margin. Host has 121 GB
# with ~50 GB available and 0 swap in use, so this leaves ~18 GB free — sizing
# to the measured working set rather than to all free RAM, because this cache
# competes with the page cache and with whatever else the operator runs.
# Re-measure with the eviction grep above before raising it further.
#
# FLAG DRIFT: upstream renamed --checkpoint-every-n-tokens (old b8780 spelling,
# which this profile used until 2026-07-28) to --checkpoint-min-step. An unknown
# flag makes llama-server exit 1, and systemd then burns its restart budget
# looping on it. Re-check every flag here against `llama-server --help` whenever
# LLAMA_BIN moves.
LLAMA_EXTRA_ARGS=--metrics --jinja --mlock --prio 2 --reasoning auto --reasoning-format deepseek --reasoning-budget 8000 --ctx-checkpoints 64 --checkpoint-min-step 2048 --cache-ram 32768
